Name: closeEndedProcesses

Type: property

Syntax: set the closeEndedProcesses to {true | false}

Syntax: get the closeEndedProcesses

Summary:
Specifies whether a <process> opened for reading that has ended leaves
the <openProcesses> once its output has been read to the end.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop, server

Example:
set the closeEndedProcesses to true

Example:
set the closeEndedProcesses to true
open process "/usr/bin/whoami" for read
read from process "/usr/bin/whoami" until EOF
put it into tUser
-- the process has ended and its output is read: it is no longer
-- among the openProcesses, and needs no close process

Value (bool):
The <closeEndedProcesses> is true or false. By default, it is false.

Description:
Use the <closeEndedProcesses> property to have a <process> that has
finished leave the <openProcesses> and the <openProcessIDs> by itself,
without a <close process>.

A <process> opened with the <open process> <command> for write, or for
neither, leaves the <openProcesses> as soon as it exits. One opened for
read or for update stays there after it exits, so that the output it
left behind can still be read; its ID in the <openProcessIDs> is then 0.
By default it stays until a <close process> closes it, even after its
output has been read to the end, as it has since LiveCode 7. Scripts
may rely on that: an ID of 0 says that the <process> has exited.

On Windows a <process> opened for read or update has always left the
list by itself once it has exited and its output has been read, so the
<closeEndedProcesses> changes nothing there: setting it makes Linux and
macOS behave as Windows does.

When the <closeEndedProcesses> is true, such a <process> leaves the
<openProcesses> and the <openProcessIDs> once it has exited and a
<read from process> has taken its output to the end (the <result> is
"eof"). Until then it stays, with its ID 0, so that no output is lost.
A <process> that is still running stays, whatever a read gives. A
<close process> of a <process> that has left the list does nothing, and
the <result> is "process is not open".

The <closeEndedProcesses> is not saved anywhere: a <stack> that wants
it sets it each time it runs.

References: openProcesses (function), openProcessIDs (function),
open process (command), read from process (command),
close process (command), kill (command), result (function),
process (glossary)
